* ============================================================================
* workspace_wbak_scan.dts -- the backup sidecars a destructive verb leaves
* behind become TABLES to the next directory scan.
*
* Status: CANDIDATE, unregistered. Not in kRegressionSpecs; run explicitly with
*   dottalkpp --script <path>            (or DO workspace_wbak_scan)
*   datarun.ps1 -CommandLines 'DO workspace_wbak_scan'
* Authored 2026-08-12 by member.ai.claude.cowork after the finding surfaced in
* the maintainer's own console: a routine WORKSPACE OPEN DBF over the x64 root
* opened 'FMGRTST.__fldbak.dbf' as Area 5.
*
* THE INTERACTION. Two features, each defensible alone:
*
*   WORKSPACE WRITEBACK ... CONFIRM preserves every file it replaces as
*   <name>.__wbak, in the target root. That is the undo of last resort and it
*   is proven recoverable (WB_T9 in workspace_writeback.dts).
*
*   WORKSPACE OPEN DBF scans the DBF slot and opens every .dbf it finds.
*
* Jointly: the directory a destructive verb writes INTO is the directory the
* scanner reads FROM, so each CONFIRM seeds N more tables for the next scan.
* Measured on the 13-table MCC posture: the second writeback made the scan
* open 26 areas. Worse than the count -- each backup attached the LIVE table's
* index (Area 12: 'ROOMS.__wbak.dbf' [index: ROOMS.cdx, attached]), an index
* built over different records, so an ordered read or SEEK on a backup follows
* an ordering that was never built for it AND reports success.
*
* WHY IT COMPOUNDS: WORKSPACE SAVE <name> MEMO MINIDB walks every OPEN area.
* writeback -> OPEN DBF -> SAVE MEMO MINIDB therefore mints a container whose
* posture DECLARES the backups as members, and the next cycle backs those up
* too. The manifest inherits the contamination, which is the one place this
* subsystem cannot tolerate it -- the posture is the enumeration authority.
*
* WHAT THIS SPEC ASSERTS, AND WHAT IT ONLY DEMONSTRATES. Stated rather than
* implied, because the difference is the whole point:
*
*   ASSERTED BY VALUE -- the PRODUCER half. WB_S1/WB_S2 prove that a confirmed
*   writeback deposits .__wbak files into the target root beside the live
*   tables, and WB_S3 proves such a file is a fully valid, openable table (not
*   an inert blob the scanner might reasonably skip). That is exactly why the
*   scanner opens it.
*
*   DEMONSTRATED BY TRANSCRIPT ONLY -- the CONSUMER half. That WORKSPACE OPEN
*   DBF then opens them is not asserted here. Read the area list this spec
*   prints; the AREA / DBAREA / DBAREAS commands report the same thing
*   interactively.
*
*   WHY IT IS NOT ASSERTED, stated carefully because the first draft of this
*   header got it wrong. It is NOT that the names do not exist -- RECNO is
*   declared in expr_tuple_glue.hpp:115. It is that they do not RESOLVE.
*   Measured 2026-08-12 on this build, three contexts, one binary:
*     ? "x" + STR(RECNO())          -> empty
*     TUPLE RECNO,LNAME  (at rec 3) -> "" | Ramirez   (LNAME resolves; RECNO does not)
*     SQLSEL COUNT FOR RECNO > 195  -> [rec 199] RECNO="" => false ... 0
*   The last is the dangerous one: no error, every record false, a confident
*   zero. DELETED and ALIAS differ -- they hit rhs_eval's bare-string fallback
*   and return their own NAMES, so ? "x" + DELETED prints "DELETED" and every
*   comparison against it reads .F.
*
*   NOT a blanket claim that RECNO is broken -- it resolves fine as a
*   PROJECTION. SMARTLIST prints a RECNO column per row, and SMARTLIST is the
*   cursor-controlled listing command to reach for (LIST is a developer
*   command with a MANUAL cursor -- its own contract says "mutates_cursor:
*   temporary during scan / cursor_restore: best effort", and its footer
*   reports the scan position by design, not the session's). The gap is
*   narrower and more specific: RECNO resolves in projection and NOT as a
*   predicate identifier, and the mismatch is silent. Control measured in the
*   same run: SMARTLIST ... FOR GPA >= 3.98 returns 2 records; SMARTLIST ...
*   FOR RECNO > 195 returns 0, with no error and a confident count.
*
*   Both shapes report absence rather than failure, which is why this spec
*   asserts FIELD VALUES instead, the same
*   discipline IDXSTALE records ("RECNO() and FOUND() render EMPTY in a '?'
*   marker and STR() does not rescue them") and the same reason the writeback
*   lane proved its enumeration defect with a file-count oracle rather than by
*   interrogating the session.
*
* Disposable: writes to dbf/wbscan, ERASE DIR'd at bootstrap AND teardown.
* Requires the mcc_minidb_memo catalog row.
* ============================================================================
SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

* --- bootstrap: clean slate -------------------------------------------------
ERASE DIR DBF/wbscan CONFIRM

FORMULA "WBAK-SCAN-PROOF-BEGIN"

* --- first writeback: lands the workspace, nothing to replace yet -----------
DO mem
WORKSPACE LOAD mcc_minidb_memo MEMO RAM
WORKSPACE WRITEBACK mcc_minidb_memo TO DBF/wbscan
WORKSPACE CLOSE
VDISK UNMOUNT

? "WB_S0_first_pass_left_no_backups:" + (FILE("DBF/wbscan/STUDENTS.__wbak.dbf") = .F.)

* --- second writeback WITH CONFIRM: every replaced file is preserved --------
DO mem
WORKSPACE LOAD mcc_minidb_memo MEMO RAM
WORKSPACE WRITEBACK mcc_minidb_memo TO DBF/wbscan CONFIRM
WORKSPACE CLOSE
VDISK UNMOUNT

* The producer, by value: backups now sit in the same root as the live tables.
? "WB_S1_confirm_minted_wbak:" + FILE("DBF/wbscan/STUDENTS.__wbak.dbf")
* Bare AND, not .AND. -- the dotted xBase-canonical forms .AND./.OR./.NOT. are
* NOT accepted by this evaluator and fail with "FORMULA error: scalar
* evaluation failed", a message naming neither the operator nor the fix.
* Measured 2026-08-12 while authoring this spec; the first draft used .AND.
* and lost a marker to it. Bare AND / OR / NOT all work.
? "WB_S2_wbak_sits_beside_live:" + (FILE("DBF/wbscan/STUDENTS.dbf") AND FILE("DBF/wbscan/STUDENTS.__wbak.dbf"))

* --- the backup is a REAL table, which is why a scanner cannot ignore it ----
DO x64
SET PATH DBF DBF/wbscan
SET PATH INDEXES DBF/wbscan/indexes
SELECT 1
USE STUDENTS.__wbak.dbf NOINDEX
GO 1
? "WB_S3_wbak_opens_and_reads:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) > "")
CLOSE

* --- the consumer half: READ THE AREA LIST BELOW ----------------------------
* No assertion is possible here -- see the header. The area list is the
* evidence: every '*.__wbak.dbf' line is a backup the scan admitted as a
* table, and every '[index: <LIVE>.cdx, attached]' beside one is a backup
* bound to an index built over different records.
WORKSPACE OPEN DBF
WORKSPACE

FORMULA "WBAK-SCAN-PROOF-END"

* --- teardown: nothing left on disk -----------------------------------------
WORKSPACE CLOSE
ERASE DIR DBF/wbscan CONFIRM
DO x64
